---
import DocumentSection from "../components/DocumentSection.astro";
import DocumentLayout from "../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro";
---

<DocumentLayout
  page="privacy"
  title="Privacy — Baros"
  description="Privacy Policy for Baros."
  heading="Privacy Policy"
  intro="This policy explains how Baros handles workout data, account data, sync, export, and deletion."
  lastUpdated="November 22, 2026"
>
  <DocumentSection title="Summary">
    <p>
      You can use Baros locally without creating an account. Signing in enables
      sync through Clerk authentication and Convex account-backed storage. You
      can export workout history and delete account-backed or local data from
      Settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      Baros does not use third-party advertising, cross-app tracking, or product
      analytics in the v1 release.
    </p>
  </DocumentSection>

  <DocumentSection title="Data stored locally">
    <p>
      Baros stores workout-related data such as exercises, sets, workout
      sessions, notes, settings, and workout history. You can use the app locally
      on your iPhone without creating an account.
    </p>
  </DocumentSection>

  <DocumentSection title="Account and authentication">
    <p>
      If you create an account, Baros uses Clerk for authentication. Clerk may
      process account information such as your email address, authentication
      identifiers, session information, and Sign in with Apple details.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you use Sign in with Apple and choose Hide My Email, Apple may provide
      a private relay email address instead of your personal email address.
    </p>
  </DocumentSection>

  <DocumentSection title="Sync">
    <p>
      When you sign in, Baros uses Convex to sync supported workout, exercise,
      settings, and history data for your account. Sync data is scoped to your
      authenticated account.
    </p>
  </DocumentSection>

  <DocumentSection title="Apple Health">
    <p>
      If you turn on Save Workouts to Apple Health in Settings, Baros asks for
      permission to save workouts, heart rate, and active energy to Apple Health,
      and to read heart rate. Each workout you start is recorded as a strength
      workout in Apple Health, on your iPhone or, if you use the Baros Apple Watch
      app, on your Apple Watch. During a workout, Baros shows your live heart rate
      from your Apple Watch or a supported sensor such as AirPods Pro 3.
    </p>
    <p>
      Health data stays on your devices. Baros never uploads heart rate or other
      Health data to its servers, never syncs it with your Baros account, and
      never uses it for advertising, marketing, or analytics. Apple Health keeps
      its own copy of your workouts, which you control in the Health app.
    </p>
    <p>
      Editing a workout in Baros updates its Health copy, and deleting a workout
      in Baros deletes its Health copy. Heart-rate readings recorded during the
      workout stay in Apple Health. Signing out, deleting local data, or deleting
      your Baros account doesn't remove workouts already saved to Apple Health;
      you can delete them in the Health app. You can turn the setting off at any
      time, or remove Baros's access in the Health app.
    </p>
  </DocumentSection>

  <DocumentSection title="Exporting and deleting your data">
    <ul>
      <li>You can export workout history from Settings.</li>
      <li>
        Signed-in users can delete their account and account-backed data from
        Settings under Privacy &amp; Data.
      </li>
      <li>
        Signed-out users can delete local data stored on the device from Settings
        under Privacy &amp; Data.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </DocumentSection>

  <DocumentSection title="No analytics, ads, or tracking">
    <p>
      Baros does not use third-party advertising, cross-app tracking, or product
      analytics. Standard platform and provider diagnostics may be processed by
      Apple, Clerk, Convex, Sentry, or hosting providers to keep Baros and its
      services reliable and secure.
    </p>
  </DocumentSection>

  <DocumentSection title="Crash and sync diagnostics">
    <p>
      Baros uses Sentry to receive crash, app-hang, and classified synchronization
      diagnostics from production builds. These diagnostics can include the app
      version and build, operating-system and device technical details, the sync
      phase and outcome, and bounded retry or pending-change counts.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you are signed in, Baros sends Sentry a one-way pseudonymous code so the
      maintainer can count how many distinct accounts are affected. Baros does not
      send Sentry your raw account identifier, email address, display name,
      authentication token, workout names, workout notes, set notes, record
      identifiers, request bodies, or full synchronization entries. The
      pseudonymous code is cleared from the active diagnostic scope when the
      current account changes or signs out.
    </p>
  </DocumentSection>

  <DocumentSection title="Contact">
    <p>
      For privacy questions or support, email <a
        href="mailto:support@tatooles.dev">support@tatooles.dev</a
      >.
    </p>
  </DocumentSection>
</DocumentLayout>
